body=f'''
<header><div class="eyebrow">Armenia · PISA 2025 · Updated empirical report · 13 November 2026</div><h1>Household resources and grade-associated learning in Armenia</h1><p class="subtitle">An overlap-based redesign comparing grades 9 and 10</p></header>
<section class="lead"><h2>What changed—and what the new estimates show</h2><p>The redesigned forests no longer produce the enormous Armenian estimates in the initial regional analysis. The main comparison uses <strong>1,758 students in 80 schools</strong>, including 348 ninth-graders. It conditions on monthly age, possessions, school mean possessions, sex and home language, and gives most weight to covariate profiles with greater grade overlap.</p><p>The estimated grade-10 versus grade-9 differences are <strong>7.7 science points, −1.1 mathematics points and 12.2 reading points</strong>. The science and mathematics intervals include zero; reading has a positive pointwise interval. None of the six primary high-versus-low possessions contrasts provides clear evidence of moderation. Adding parental education or occupation separately also yields no clear moderation evidence, with especially wide intervals for occupation.</p><p>This is an improvement in numerical stability and transparency, not a recovery of national causal annual learning effects. The main sample represents about <strong>24.9% of the original weighted Armenian PISA sample</strong>. A stricter school-support rule changes the comparison population and shifts the estimates. Residual age imbalance and selective parental nonresponse remain consequential.</p></section>
<nav><a href="#design">1 Design</a><a href="#sample">2 Sample and overlap</a><a href="#results">3 Forest results</a><a href="#parents">4 Parental inputs</a><a href="#checks">5 Sensitivity</a><a href="#interpretation">6 Interpretation</a><a href="#appendix">Appendices</a></nav>
<section id="design"><h2>1. Research question and revised design</h2><p>The question is whether grade-associated differences in science, mathematics and reading vary with students’ household resources and the resources of their schoolmates. Grade remains a proxy for additional schooling exposure. PISA observes different students at one time, so the estimates are conditional grade comparisons, not observed individual learning growth.</p><p>The original Armenian forest combined ESCS complete cases, several grade levels, school mean grade as a predictor, and an unrestricted average of continuous-treatment scores. The redesign changes all four choices. ESCS is absent from the new forest models. Possessions is the primary socioeconomic measure; parental education and occupation enter separately in supplementary models that retain possessions.</p><p>The exposure is binary: W=1 for grade 10 and W=0 for grade 9. The main model uses HOMEPOS, its school mean, recorded sex, home-language indicator and monthly-age indicators. Supplementary models add either PAREDINT and its school mean or HISEI and its school mean. School mean grade is excluded: it can nearly reveal grade in schools dominated by one cohort. No unobserved school effects are thereby controlled; a school fixed-effects benchmark addresses that separate concern.</p><p>For each socioeconomic variable, the school mean uses all Armenian PISA respondents with that variable observed, weighted by the final student weight. It is not recomputed on the smaller parental complete-case sample. These are sampled school-context measures, not census peer characteristics. They include the focal student, and uncertainty in their construction is not fully propagated.</p><h3>Overlap target</h3><p>Let e(X) denote the estimated probability of grade 10 given the included covariates. The target averages conditional grade differences with weights proportional to w×e(X)×[1−e(X)], where w is the PISA student weight. This gives less influence to profiles for which grade is almost predetermined. It changes the population of interest; it cannot recover the missing counterfactuals for excluded students.</p><div class="equation">Overlap target: E[w e(X)(1−e(X)) τ(X)] / E[w e(X)(1−e(X))]</div><p>Honest, school-clustered regression forests estimate e(X) without outcomes, using out-of-bag predictions. Honest binary causal forests are then fitted for every plausible value, with 2,000 trees and a target minimum leaf size of 15. A single propensity estimate per specification is shared across the ten plausible values. The reported overlap averages use GRF’s residual-regression aggregation, rather than unrestricted inverse-propensity scores. Subgroup averages use the same method within prespecified reporting groups. This implementation follows <a href="https://grf-labs.github.io/grf/reference/average_treatment_effect.html">GRF’s overlap-target documentation</a>.</p><p>The algorithm’s name does not establish causality. A causal interpretation would still require grade placement to be conditionally independent of unobserved achievement determinants. Entry timing, prior achievement, school transitions and retention can violate that condition. Restricting schools by their observed grade composition also defines a selected descriptive population, not a randomized or externally assigned eligibility group.</p></section>
<section id="sample"><h2>2. Which students and schools are represented?</h2><p>Armenia has 6,804 sampled students; 6,755 are in grades 9 and 10. Requiring observed possessions, monthly age, sex and home language leaves 5,390. Requiring at least two observed students in each grade within the analytic school sample leaves 1,760 students in 80 schools. The 0.10–0.90 propensity rule removes two additional students, leaving 1,758. Every retained school must still contain at least one student in each grade after trimming.</p>{coverage}<p class="note">Table 1. Actual sample sizes and coverage. Weighted coverage is relative to all Armenian PISA students, including grades outside 9–10. Overlap effective N is calculated from w×e×(1−e); it is not a count of independent schools. Matched models reuse their corresponding parental model’s exact rows but re-estimate propensity and target weights.</p><p>The main retained propensities range from 0.54 to 0.90. Grade 10 remains more common throughout; overlap does not require equal raw numbers in the two grades. The stricter sensitivity requires five students in each grade before propensity trimming and retains only 681 students in 28 schools, representing 9.2% of the original weighted sample.</p><p>Parental information creates an additional selection layer. Possessions plus education retains 865 students in 55 schools, covering 12.2% of the original weighted sample. Possessions plus occupation retains 488 students in 42 schools, covering 7.0%. These losses are a reason to keep possessions as the main measure rather than require all parental components jointly.</p><p>These are support rules for this exploratory redesign, chosen before examining its outcome estimates. They are not universal thresholds or a preregistered design. A cell with both grades observed may still have weak conditional overlap. The complete sample flow, estimated probability ranges, group counts and cutpoints are retained in the accompanying CSV files.</p></section>
<section id="results"><h2>3. Grade differences and possessions heterogeneity</h2>{main_table}<p class="note">Table 2. Main overlap-targeted forest averages and school fixed-effects benchmarks on the same students and estimated target weights. Brackets contain 95% t intervals combining school-clustered sampling uncertainty and ten plausible values. The benchmark additionally conditions on school and age-category fixed effects and includes individual possessions, sex and language. Its constant slope and the forest’s flexible overlap average are not identical estimands.</p><p>The reading estimate is positive in the main comparison; science is imprecisely positive and mathematics is close to zero. The reading test also survives Holm adjustment across these three main domain averages, but that does not establish robustness to changing the school-support population. The school fixed-effects estimates are 5.1 science points, −3.2 mathematics points and 10.7 reading points, broadly consistent with the main pattern.</p><p>Heterogeneity is summarized in three groups rather than ten deciles. Possessions cutpoints are weighted tertiles in the main school-supported sample before probability trimming. The same cutpoints are retained in the sensitivity models. School groups divide students by school mean possessions; they do not divide schools into equal counts. Ties are kept together.</p>{fg}<p>All six primary high-minus-low contrasts—student and school possessions across the three domains—have wide intervals and Holm-adjusted p-values of 1.00. For science, the contrast is −7.6 points for student possessions (95% interval −31.0 to 15.7) and −1.7 points for school possessions (−27.9 to 24.5). These intervals accommodate materially different slopes. The result is insufficient evidence of moderation, not evidence that resources do not matter.</p><p>These group averages describe different overlap populations. Even when both student and school resources enter the forest, the curves do not isolate the effect of changing resources while holding every other characteristic fixed. They should not be interpreted as the effect of providing possessions or moving a child to a wealthier school.</p></section>
<section id="parents"><h2>4. Parental education and occupation, estimated separately</h2><p>The education model includes possessions plus parental education; the occupation model includes possessions plus parental occupation. Each also includes the corresponding school means. Education and occupation are never jointly required. The reporting groups preserve ties; student parental education has two observed groups, ≤12 years and >12 through 16 years; the proposed >16-year group is empty. Its contrast therefore compares group 2 with group 1. School-mean parental education retains three groups using separate cutpoints. Actual group sizes are shown in Appendix A.</p>{fp}<p>None of the 12 high-versus-low parental contrasts survives the parental-family Holm correction. Their unadjusted intervals are also wide. The occupation contrasts are particularly imprecise, consistent with the small and selected sample. Differences between school and student curves should not be promoted into mechanisms on the basis of their point estimates.</p><p>The same-student comparisons help distinguish an adjustment change from a sample change. In science, the education model estimates 7.57 points, compared with 7.61 when only possessions is included on those same students. The occupation model estimates 0.40 points, compared with 3.28 in its matched possessions-only model. For reading, the occupation model estimates −0.25 points versus 2.60 in the matched model; both are imprecise. These comparisons do not support the claim that replacing ESCS necessarily uncovers a large hidden grade gain.</p><p>Matched rows do not mean identical statistical targets: adding a parental variable changes estimated grade probabilities and hence the overlap weights as well as the adjustment model. These differences are descriptive specification checks, not formal tests of a pure measurement effect. The parental components may still moderate gains in ways that the available sample cannot detect.</p></section>
<section id="checks"><h2>5. Sensitivity, balance and uncertainty</h2>{fr}<p>Relaxing the propensity bounds from 0.10–0.90 to 0.05–0.95 has almost no effect: it restores only two students. Requiring five students per grade changes the school population much more substantially. The resulting estimates are −4.8 science points, −10.9 mathematics points and 4.4 reading points, each with an interval spanning zero. The intervals overlap the main estimates; this is not a formal finding that the estimates differ. It does show that the main reading result should not be presented as established across all supported Armenian school populations.</p><p>Forest random-seed variation is small in the limited numerical check: using two additional seeds for science plausible value 1 yields 6.81 and 6.66 points. This check evaluates computational stability for that outcome draw, not identification or robustness across populations.</p>{fb}<p>Overlap weighting improves measured balance, but does not eliminate imbalance. The largest absolute standardized difference in the main model falls from 0.571 to 0.275; age is the largest remaining imbalance. Four measured covariates or age indicators remain above 0.10. The parental occupation model has a remaining maximum difference of 0.544. These diagnostics reinforce the exploratory interpretation and the importance of outcome adjustment; they do not certify conditional exchangeability.</p><h3>What the intervals include</h3><p>All three domains use all ten plausible values. Sampling uncertainty is clustered by school; between-plausible-value uncertainty is added using Rubin’s variance formula. The t degrees of freedom are capped at the relevant number of schools minus one and at the imputation-based degrees of freedom. This widens intervals for the 28-school strict-support analysis and for smaller reporting groups.</p><p>The intervals do not include a complete refit under all 80 PISA replicate weights, nor full uncertainty from estimating propensity scores, choosing the supported sample, estimating school means or determining group cutpoints. They are conditional exploratory intervals. Student sampling weights are used in estimation, but that does not make these intervals fully design-based PISA inference. Parent-only subgroup claims should be especially restrained.</p><p>Six possessions contrasts form the primary heterogeneity family; twelve parental-input contrasts form a separate exploratory family. Holm corrections are reported within those families. Sensitivity contrasts form an additional family in the output files. A reporting group is flagged if it has fewer than 20 students in either grade or fewer than ten schools. One strict-support school-possessions group fails this diagnostic. These flags are warnings, not proofs that the other groups are adequately powered.</p></section>
<section id="interpretation"><h2>6. What can now be said about Armenia?</h2><p><strong>The most defensible advance is a cleaner comparison population, not a larger estimated schooling effect.</strong> The large negative values from the initial unrestricted forest do not recur. That change cannot be attributed solely to removing ESCS: the exposure, sample, predictors and aggregation method all changed.</p><p>Household possessions offers a more usable main socioeconomic measure because it retains more observations and allows parental background to be studied without forcing joint complete cases. Yet the supported mixed-grade school population is a minority of the national PISA sample. School organization at the transition between grades 9 and 10 remains part of the substantive research question, rather than a nuisance that can be removed mechanically.</p><p>The present evidence supports a positive reading association in the main overlap population and considerable uncertainty about science, mathematics and socioeconomic moderation. It does not support a confident story that ESCS had concealed large annual learning gains, or that parental inputs do not matter. A useful next identification step would verify school-entry timing and school transitions and improve pre-grade achievement information. Simply growing more trees or reporting more SES groups would not supply those missing comparisons.</p><p>The report preserves the distinction between accumulated achievement and grade-associated differences. The descriptive achievement and within/between-school summaries below provide context; their school-level variance shares are not estimates of school effectiveness or policy leverage.</p></section>
<section id="appendix"><h2>Appendices and replication</h2><details><summary>A. Every subgroup estimate and its observed support</summary><p>These tables include sensitivity models. “Adequate” means passing the stated count diagnostic, not established causal overlap or statistical power. Group intervals are pointwise. The strict-support middle school-possessions group contains only seven schools.</p>{group_table}</details><details><summary>B. Primary and parental high-minus-low tests</summary>{contrast_table}</details><details><summary>C. Achievement levels and within/between-school variance</summary><p>Descriptive quantities use final student weights and average the statistic over ten plausible values. Selected-sample values here use ordinary student weights, not the overlap target weights. Variance shares are exact weighted sample partitions and include sampling noise in school means. No sampling intervals are supplied for this contextual appendix.</p>{variance_table}{levels_table}</details><details><summary>D. Balance, cutpoints and numerical checks</summary>{balance_table}{table(mapped(cuts))}{table(seed.round(3))}<p>Reported overlap averages are checked against GRF’s built-in estimator. Joint subgroup regressions reproduce separate subgroup point estimates and retain cross-group covariance at school level. A separate NumPy calculation checks the main science, mathematics and reading PV1 overlap coefficients and clustered standard errors; additional checks verify pooling, multiplicity correction, sample support and report contents.</p></details><details><summary>E. Reproduction and scope</summary><pre>python3 02_scripts/caucasus_central_asia/02_extract.py
Rscript 02_scripts/caucasus_central_asia/13_armenia_redesign.R
Rscript 02_scripts/caucasus_central_asia/14_armenia_diagnostics.R
python3 02_scripts/caucasus_central_asia/15_armenia_report.py
python3 02_scripts/caucasus_central_asia/16_armenia_validate.py</pre><p>Original input: OECD <a href="https://www.oecd.org/en/data/datasets/pisa-2025-database.html">PISA 2025 student public-use file</a>, <code>01_data/CY09_MS_STU_PUF.sav</code>. Derived student records stay under <code>01_data/derived/caucasus_central_asia/armenia_redesign</code> and are excluded from the shareable bundle. Aggregate results, figures and software versions are under <code>03_outputs/caucasus_central_asia/armenia_redesign</code>. All computations are restricted to Armenia. Other countries’ existing estimates are untouched.</p><p>This report supersedes the Armenia forest interpretation in the earlier regional report. The earlier regional HTML is preserved as <code>report_regional_before_armenia_redesign.html</code>. The new report is saved both as <code>armenia_redesign.html</code> and as the active <code>report.html</code>. This exploratory redesign was not preregistered. No simulated empirical results or imputed ESCS values are used.</p></details></section>
<footer>Sources: local OECD public-use data and embedded variable labels; <a href="https://grf-labs.github.io/grf/reference/average_treatment_effect.html">GRF overlap aggregation</a> and the installed implementation; <a href="https://grf-labs.github.io/grf/reference/causal_forest.html">GRF causal forest documentation</a>. Numerical results are newly estimated for this Armenian redesign.</footer>
'''
css='''*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#f3f3ef;color:#1e313b;font-family:Georgia,serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.65}main{max-width:1200px;margin:auto;padding:48px 55px;background:white}header{border-top:7px solid #087e8b;padding:30px 0}h1{font-size:44px;line-height:1.16;margin:18px 0}h2{font-size:28px;line-height:1.3}h3{font-size:22px}.eyebrow{font:12px Arial,sans-serif;letter-spacing:1.5px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#087e8b}.subtitle{font-size:23px;color:#536874}.lead{background:#eaf4f3;border-left:4px solid #087e8b;padding:25px!important}section{padding:25px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #dce3e6}nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:18px;padding:24px 0;font:13px Arial,sans-serif}a{color:#006d79}figure{margin:28px 0}img{width:100%;height:auto}figcaption,.note,footer{font:13px/1.55 Arial,sans-serif;color:#526671}table{border-collapse:collapse;font:12px/1.5 Arial,sans-serif;width:100%}td,th{padding:9px;border-bottom:1px solid #dce3e6;text-align:left}th{background:#edf2f4}tr:nth-child(even){background:#f7f9fa}.table-wrap{overflow:auto;margin:22px 0}details{border:1px solid #dce3e6;padding:18px;margin:20px 0}summary{font:600 15px Arial,sans-serif;cursor:pointer}code{font-size:.84em;background:#f3f5f6;padding:2px}pre{font-size:12px;padding:15px;background:#f3f5f6;overflow:auto}.equation{padding:20px;background:#f4f6f8;text-align:center}footer{padding:25px 0}@media(max-width:700px){main{padding:22px}body{font-size:16px}h1{font-size:33px}}@media print{nav{display:none}main{padding:0}figure{break-inside:avoid}}'''
